course: dictionary
lesson: 554
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Attraction is one of them. To find the right meaning, read the words around it, because they are its context and they leave clues.
explain: «Geckos stick to glass with millions of tiny toe hairs that cling by very weak attractions between molecules, and scientists have made tape that copies them.» Here attraction is a pulling force that holds things together. The words cling and stick tell you this is a science meaning.
explain: In a different sentence, «The old castle is the main attraction in the town» means a place people want to visit. If a story says «Mia felt a strong attraction to the new girl», it means a feeling of liking someone and being drawn to her. Always try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Attraction is a noun, and it can have more than one meaning.
fact: An attraction can be a place or thing people want to visit, like a tourist attraction.
fact: Attraction can also mean a feeling of liking someone or something and being drawn to them.
fact: In science, attraction is a force that pulls things together, like a magnet and iron, or gravity.
fact: In magnets, opposite poles show attraction, and like poles push each other away.
exercise: «Geckos stick to glass with millions of tiny toe hairs that cling by very weak attractions between molecules, and scientists have made tape that copies them.» In this sentence, what does attraction mean? | *A force that pulls things together | A place people want to visit | A loud noise | A feeling of liking someone
exercise: «The old castle is the main attraction in the town, and thousands of visitors come each summer.» What does attraction mean here? | A kind of weather | *A place people want to visit | A feeling of being drawn to someone | A force that pulls iron
exercise: Mia says, «I felt a strong attraction to the new girl in our class.» What does attraction mean here? | A school trip | A magnet pulling a nail | *A feeling of liking her and being drawn to her | A place to visit
test: In science, what does attraction mean? | A place where people go on holiday | A feeling of being sad | *A force that pulls things together, like a magnet and iron | A loud sound made by a machine
test: «The tourist attraction drew crowds of visitors every weekend.» What is the best meaning of attraction here? | A pulling force between molecules | A feeling of liking someone | *A place people want to visit | A kind of bright light
exam: «The magnet and the iron nail felt a strong magnetic attraction.» Which meaning fits best, and what clue tells you? | A kind of weather, because the magnet was cold | *A pulling force, because the magnet and the nail are things that pull together | A place people want to visit, because magnets are used in museums | A feeling of liking, because the nail felt happy
